WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.273 --> 00:00:02.690
(slow music)

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:08.040
- [Narrator] This is a red abalone.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.480
It's basically the oceans'
version of a garden snail.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.690
It lives primarily on large rocks

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.700
in the lush kelp forests of California.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.280
It's also been a popular delicacy

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.790
in the state for over a century.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.890
While wild red abalone is not available

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.430
to commercial fishermen,

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:27.290
recreational abalone divers
in northern California

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:30.570
can snorkel down to the
rocks, pry off a snail,

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.780
and bring it home for a barbecue.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.323
That is until recently.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.760
Something is happening to the kelp forests

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.130
and it's threatening the
survival of the species

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.050
and the northern California communities

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.140
that rely on the red abalone for business.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.190
In December of 2018
California Fish and Game

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:50.160
decided to close down the recreational

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.630
abalone fishery until 2021

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:55.310
in hopes that they give
this sea snail population

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:56.323
a chance to rebound.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:00.613
- My name is Joe Cresalia.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:06.273
I've been diving since 1969, 50 years.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.923
The North California coast,

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.803
it's just an incredibly beautiful place.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.930
The topography, the really high cliffs,

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.510
the big rocks that stick out of the water,

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:21.170
it's almost prehistoric in that way.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:24.413
You feel like you've really
gone off into another world.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.250
What we're catching up
here, the red abalone,

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.900
there's a variety of abalone,

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.650
but the red abalone traditionally

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.500
is what your grandparents would think of

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.123
if they were seeing abalone on the coast.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.610
For people that think they don't like fish

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.770
or people that don't
think they like shellfish,

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.290
it's amazing to see
their eyes just sparkle

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:51.187
when they take a bit of it and go,

00:01:51.187 --> 00:01:53.080
"This is absolutely incredible."

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.480
And you know before they took the bite

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.830
they were almost afraid to take a bite.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:59.300
- [Narrator] In 1916
California established

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.870
a commercial fishery for wild red abalone.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.880
But in 1997 commercial fishery closed

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.763
due to a dwindling population.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.530
20 years later the
recreational fishery closed

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.240
when purple sea urchins
ravaged the kelp forests

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.903
and left the red abalone
dying of starvation.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.890
- I appreciate what Fish and Game has done

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.150
and I think anybody intelligent does

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.000
and it's recently closing it down

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.940
with something I had predicted
by watching the ocean.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:32.960
There's a couple of places,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.310
I've literally been diving for 50 years,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.570
and there really wasn't enough kelp.

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:38.513
You can see it.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.470
You would see the kelp stumps

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.980
and four or five sea urchins
on it just chomping it.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.380
It looked like someone had come in

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.320
with a chainsaw and just cut it.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:52.430
You're going down and you're seeing

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.140
like a blanket of sea urchins.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:55.390
The kelp was disappearing,

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:57.310
the abalone you weren't seeing as many of

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.680
'cause they were off looking for food,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.483
and there was an unbalance.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.940
I wear a dive watch that gives me

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.320
my depth and my water temperature

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.090
and I was noticing the water
temperature getting higher,

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.367
and I was used to the water being

00:03:12.367 --> 00:03:15.040
49, 51, kind of in that range,

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.610
sometimes it'd be in a little colder,

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.020
it'd be a little warmer,

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.430
but I was seeing spikes where the water

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:23.493
was 54 or 55 degrees at times.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.620
For 40 years I'd seen pretty much

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.090
the same type of temperate consistently,

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:33.490
and to see those types of
variances made me wonder.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:36.240
(waves crashing)

00:03:37.204 --> 00:03:39.293
The bottom's changing,
there's no question about it,

00:03:39.293 --> 00:03:42.420
and you could see old abalone
shells broken and stuff

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.620
where abalone have died.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:49.373
So yeah,

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.090
life's changed on the bottom.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.629
It's going through a change.

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:01.046
(slow music)

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.680
- My name is Dr. Laura Rogers-Bennet

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:16.080
and I am a Senior Environmental Scientist

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.723
here at the Bodega Marine Laboratory.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:22.780
Abalone are very much an indicator

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.983
of the health of the kelp forest.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:30.600
They are very sensitive to
any changes in food abundance.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.540
They're not able to
switch over and eat meat,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.330
and so when their numbers go down

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:41.270
we know there's something
going wrong in the kep forest.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:43.770
(slow music)

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.120
- In 2013 we had a sea
star wasting disease

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.240
that hit our coast really hard

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.590
and that took out a lot
of predatory sea stars.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:02.330
After that we had the warm blob

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:06.550
and the El Nino of 2014 and '15.

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:11.550
- Up in the Gulf of Alaska
there formed a warm water blob

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:16.230
and the oceanographers called it a "blob,"

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.380
and so that warm water sat in a patch

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:22.630
and extended all the
way down to our region,

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:26.300
and that feature set up and didn't move,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.773
and just stayed there.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:30.320
- [Katie] That took out all of our kelp

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.503
because the water got too
warm for our kep to survive.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.330
- [Narrator] Studies done on the blob

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:37.630
showed that while there were a number

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:39.890
of natural drivers that formed it,

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:43.343
human-generated global
warming likely made it worse.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.540
- Shortly after that we had
the purple urchin explosion

00:05:49.854 --> 00:05:52.410
of around 2015 and '16.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.830
They can mow down forests like lawnmowers,

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:57.620
and they multiply very quickly,

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:59.170
and they grow very quickly.

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:02.520
They grow a lot quicker
than species like abalone,

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.590
so the abalone can't really keep up

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.740
with their eating habits.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.950
The purple urchin are taking the kelp

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:12.070
before it can even get to a mature age,

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:16.020
so they're eating baby kelps
before they can reproduce,

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:18.040
and that's what's causing
this huge urchin barren

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:19.190
that we've been seeing.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.490
Usually in a healthy
ecosystem a red abalone

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:27.550
would be hidden under a rock.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.040
Lately we've been seeing
them on top of rocks,

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:34.130
and they're almost half to
three-quarters of their body

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.530
are perched up, and they're just waiting

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:39.760
for anything to drift by and float

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:42.840
into their muscle or their mouth.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.803
So, it's a little bit of an odd sighting.

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:51.070
Some divers have a little bit of kelp

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:55.370
they keep in their pockets in
case they pass a red abalone

00:06:56.685 --> 00:06:58.660
and you can put kelp
in front of an abalone

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:00.230
and it will lurch up.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:02.320
It's really wild to watch.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:06.140
It will actually lurch up like a puppy dog

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:07.420
that just smelled a treat

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:10.160
and it will almost unhinge its whole body

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:13.420
just to go grab that piece
of kelp, and it tucks it in,

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.410
and it kind of like holds it from

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.690
all of the other urchin
that are usually around it.

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:20.827
We collected new numbers as of 2018

00:07:20.827 --> 00:07:24.440
and we didn't see any improvement
in the abalone population.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.873
If anything we saw more declines.

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:36.730
- So we typically see around
24,000 to 26,000 divers

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:42.820
coming to dive, and rock-pick,
and collect abalone,

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:44.870
but they're also coming with a lot

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:47.350
of their friends and family.

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:51.010
It's estimated that the
recreational red abalone fishery

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:56.010
is worth about $44 million
to the local communities

00:07:56.150 --> 00:08:00.600
and to the fishermen who are
participating in the fishery.

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:02.620
- Silver's at the Wharf is a full-service

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:06.370
seafood and steak restaurant,
and 17 lodging rooms

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:09.600
which are used principally by fishermen.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:14.310
My name is Jim Hurst and we
invented Silver's at the Wharf

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.440
in Northern Harbor,
Fort Bragg, California.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:20.220
The impact of the abalone closure

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:25.220
is somewhere about
$30,000 to $40,000 a month

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:29.930
that's attributed to loss of
food sales and lodging sales.

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:32.490
Once you lose that business
you don't get it back.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:36.260
It's interesting to compare
what's going on today

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:38.800
to when our mill closed,

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:40.920
and when the mill closed
everybody thought,

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.330
oh my God, we're gonna die.

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:46.690
It's gonna have a huge impact
and it can't be replaced.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:49.660
- My name is Chris Brians,
we're here at Harvest Market,

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:54.120
I am a grocery clerk, I have
worked here for 29 years.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.110
There is a large amount of people

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:58.083
that came to Fort Bragg to go diving.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:00.963
The whole family would come.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:04.870
April first comes and goes now,

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:07.190
whereas before you could see the influx

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:09.040
of the people that were going diving.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:13.680
- I think seeing and understanding

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.970
how interconnected everything is,

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:20.380
being able to pick your food
out of the ocean like that,

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:22.340
you have an appreciation for the effort

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:25.370
that goes into it and where it comes from.

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:29.180
This isn't from some
abstract store someplace,

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.100
this is actually your activities,

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.860
and you see how it grows,
and there's a relationship.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:36.610
It should be a relationship
of stewardship.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.077
This is a source of food,
and you wanna protect it,

00:09:39.077 --> 00:09:40.410
and you wanna take care of it

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:42.150
so that you can continue to do it.

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:45.100
It's a renewable resource as
long as it's properly handled.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.350
- As the globe warms we're going

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:52.030
to have more marine heatwaves,

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:55.010
we're going to have
longer duration heatwaves,

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:58.270
and they're going to be
broader geographic extent.

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:01.790
And so I think this is really giving us

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.480
an inkling of what is to come.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:08.570
- It's quite sad that these people

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:12.563
that have done this for
generations have to stop.

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:18.430
- It's interesting, recently
I was talking to some people

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:19.377
and somebody made the comment,

00:10:19.377 --> 00:10:20.817
"Yeah, nature's gonna heal itself,

00:10:20.817 --> 00:10:22.977
"the question is will man
go along with it or not,

00:10:22.977 --> 00:10:25.957
"or will he make the environment
so intolerable for itself

00:10:25.957 --> 00:10:28.320
"that that'll be it."

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.193
I'd like to think mankind will
go on also and be part of it.

00:10:34.468 --> 00:10:37.218
(waves crashing)

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:42.680
(slow music)

